text:
This is an outstanding discussion (with Dr Anthony Restain, a psychiatrist trained at UPenn, now Chair at Cooper Medical School, and Dr Rachael Bedard a PCP) on the current state of SSRIs for depression.  A very balanced discussion. There is much unhelpful "noise" on social medial AND from our national leaders (RFK, Jr, et al.) confusing the public about both mental health, "over prescribing" of SSRIs, and issues of duration/tapering of therapy and stopping meds.  

Key elements of discussion:

1. Brain is complex, anxiety and depression are lot more complex than a simple "chemical imbalance" (whether serotonin or any other transmitter) as previously thought; it has to do with "neuroplasticity" and adaptability of circuits AND being rigid that impairs learning (major problem);

2. Behavioral and "talk therapy" are the cornerstone to optimize normal circuits (i..optimize  lifestyle such as movement and exercise, sleep, diet, avoidance of artificial stimulants/depressants such as alcohol, weed); and learn effective coping and life skills to deal with routine stress of job, money, relationships;

3. Medications, carefully monitored and personalized for side effects AND beneficial effects, often requiring trial and error; sometimes meds may be required prior to talk therapy; many niche controversies exist as to duration, how and when to stop meds;

4. Environment and family presence and support are important, but there are practical barriers and families need to know when to reach for professional help.

There is a profound shortage of mental health professionals who can provide the required expertise. There are barriers to access, insurance coverage, and still miseducation in families and the public.  This is a simple and credible discussion.

